      title: '+ Perform risk stratification for cardiac rehabilitation and identify contraindications to initiating exercise. How do you manage his acute volume overload?',
      content: '**AHA Risk Stratification for Cardiac Rehabilitation**:\n\nThis patient is **HIGH RISK** based on the following:\n- EF < 40% (EF 35%)\n- NYHA Class III heart failure\n- Post-CABG with recent atrial fibrillation\n- Abnormal hemodynamics with exercise (limited to 4.2 METs)\n- Current volume overload\n\n**High-risk patients require**:\n- **Continuous ECG telemetry monitoring** during all exercise sessions (minimum 18 sessions or until stable)\n- **Physician availability** on-site or immediately accessible\n- **Blood pressure and SpO2 monitoring** before, during, and after exercise\n- **Daily weight check** before each session (>2 lb gain in 24 hours or >5 lbs in a week warrants medical evaluation)\n\n**Contraindications to Exercise** (must be resolved before starting):\n- **Absolute contraindications**: Unstable angina, decompensated heart failure (THIS PATIENT — currently volume overloaded), acute PE, acute myocarditis/pericarditis, symptomatic severe aortic stenosis, uncontrolled arrhythmia, acute aortic dissection, resting SBP > 200 or DBP > 110.\n- **Relative contraindications**: Moderate valvular stenosis, known electrolyte abnormalities, uncontrolled metabolic disease, resting HR > 120 bpm.\n\n**Management of Acute Volume Overload** (before starting rehab):\n1. **Increase furosemide dose**: Double the current dose (e.g., furosemide 40 mg to 80 mg daily, or add an afternoon dose). Target net negative fluid balance of 1-2 liters/day until euvolemic.\n2. **Sodium restriction**: Reinforce < 2 grams/day. Dietary counseling referral.\n3. **Fluid restriction**: 1.5-2 liters/day for patients with hyponatremia or persistent congestion.\n4. **Daily weights**: Patient must weigh himself every morning before eating, after voiding, in the same clothing. Report weight gain > 2 lbs in 24 hours.\n5. **Electrolyte monitoring**: Check BMP within 48-72 hours of diuretic adjustment (risk of hypokalemia, hyponatremia, worsening renal function with aggressive diuresis)