      content: '**Exercise Prescription for Phase II Cardiac Rehabilitation (HFrEF)**:\n\n**Aerobic Training**:\n1. **Frequency**: 3 sessions/week in supervised setting. Encourage 2 additional low-intensity home sessions (walking) as tolerated.\n\n2. **Intensity** — Use multiple methods for safety:\n- **Heart Rate Reserve (HRR/Karvonen method)**: Start at 40-60% HRR, progress to 60-80% HRR.\n  - HRR = (Peak HR on GXT - Resting HR) x target % + Resting HR\n  - His values: (108 - 72) x 0.40 + 72 = 86 bpm (low end), x 0.60 + 72 = 94 bpm (high end)\n  - **Starting target HR range: 86-94 bpm**\n- **RPE (Rating of Perceived Exertion)**: Target Borg 11-14 (\"fairly light\" to \"somewhat hard\") on the 6-20 scale.\n- **METs**: Start at 2-3 METs (slow walking, stationary cycling at low resistance), progress toward his peak of 4.2 METs and beyond.\n- **Talk test**: Should be able to carry on a conversation during exercise. If unable to speak in sentences, intensity is too high.\n\n3. **Duration**: Start with 15-20 minutes of continuous aerobic exercise (or interval training if unable to sustain). Progress by 5 minutes per session every 1-2 weeks to a goal of 30-45 minutes.\n\n4. **Modalities**: Treadmill walking (preferred — most functional), recumbent bicycle (easier on sternum), arm ergometer (after sternal precautions cleared, typically 8-12 weeks), NuStep.\n\n5. **Warm-up and cool-down**: 5-10 minutes each. Gradual HR elevation and reduction. Critical in heart failure to avoid orthostatic hypotension (beta-blocker and diuretic effects).\n\n**Resistance Training** (begin after 3-4 weeks of aerobic training and sternal clearance):\n- Light resistance: 40-60% of 1-RM, 10-15 repetitions, 1-2 sets.\n- Focus on major muscle groups: quadriceps, hamstrings, biceps, latissimus.\n- **Avoid Valsalva maneuver** — exhale during exertion, inhale during relaxation.\n- Sternal precautions must be observed: no heavy lifting (>10 lbs), no chest fly or bench press until full sternal healing confirmed (typically 8-12 weeks).\n\n**Safety Parameters — When to STOP Exercise**:\n- SBP drop > 10 mmHg from baseline with increasing workload\n- SBP > 200 mmHg or DBP > 110 mmHg\n- New ST depression > 1 mm or ST elevation > 1 mm\n- New arrhythmia (VT, rapid AF, new heart block)\n- SpO2 < 88%\n- Borg dyspnea or angina > 6/10\n- Dizziness, pallor, cyanosis, confusion\n- Weight gain > 2 lbs from previous session\n\n**Progression Strategy**:\n- Weeks 1-4: Low intensity (40-50% HRR), 15-20 minutes, focus on building confidence\n- Weeks 5-8: Moderate intensity (50-60% HRR), 20-30 minutes, add resistance training\n- Weeks 9-12: Progress toward 60-70% HRR, 30-40 minutes, increase resistance\n- Weeks 13-36: Full program, target 30-45 minutes at 60-80% HRR\n\n**Evidence for Cardiac Rehab in Heart Failure** (HF-ACTION trial):\n- Modest reduction in all-cause mortality and hospitalization (11% reduction)\n- Significant improvement in quality of life, functional capacity, and depression\n- Safe — exercise training in HFrEF does NOT increase adverse events\n- The benefit is dose-dependent: higher adherence = better outcomes',
