      content: '**Phases of Cardiac Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Phase I (Inpatient)**: Begins during hospitalization. Early mobilization, patient education, risk factor counseling. Typically 3-7 days.\n\n2. **Phase II (Outpatient, Supervised)**: This is what the patient is completing. 36 sessions (up to 72 with documented medical necessity). ECG-monitored, supervised exercise. Covered by Medicare Part B (80% coverage after deductible).\n\n3. **Phase III (Maintenance/Community-Based)**: Ongoing independent exercise program. Typically at a gym, community center, or home. May or may not include periodic monitoring. **NOT covered by Medicare or most commercial insurance** — this is a major barrier.\n\n4. **Phase IV (Lifelong Maintenance)**: Self-directed, independent exercise. No supervision.\n\n**Insurance Coverage Details**:\n- **Medicare Part B**: Covers Phase II cardiac rehab for qualifying diagnoses (post-MI, post-CABG, stable angina, heart failure with EF <= 35%, post-heart transplant, post-valve repair/replacement). Patient pays 20% coinsurance.\n- **36 initial sessions**: Approved with physician referral and documented plan.\n- **Additional 36 sessions (up to 72 total)**: May be approved with documentation of continued medical necessity (ongoing symptoms, need for monitoring, continued functional improvement).\n- **Intensive Cardiac Rehab (ICR)**: 72 sessions with enhanced lifestyle modification components. Covered by Medicare for eligible programs (Pritikin, Ornish, Benson-Henry).\n\n**Transition to Phase III — Key Strategies**:\n\n1. **Graduated independence**: In the final weeks of Phase II, reduce monitoring frequency. Teach the patient to self-monitor (HR by palpation or wearable device, RPE, symptom awareness, daily weights).\n\n2. **Written home exercise prescription**: Provide a detailed, individualized exercise plan including:\n- Type: Walking 30-45 minutes (or cycling)\n- Intensity: HR range 86-108 bpm, RPE 11-14\n- Frequency: 5 days/week aerobic, 2-3 days/week resistance\n- Progression: Increase duration by 5 minutes every 2 weeks\n\n3. **Community resources**: Connect with YMCA cardiac wellness programs (many offer reduced rates for cardiac rehab graduates), Silver Sneakers (Medicare-sponsored gym access — check eligibility), local heart failure support groups.\n\n4. **Telehealth/remote monitoring**: Emerging evidence supports home-based cardiac rehab with remote ECG monitoring and video check-ins. May improve access for patients in rural areas.\n\n5. **Ongoing medical follow-up**: Quarterly visits with cardiology/heart failure clinic. Repeat echocardiogram at 3-6 months to reassess EF (if EF improves to > 35%, may not need ICD). Annual exercise stress testing to guide prescription updates.\n\n6. **Red flag education for the patient and family**: Seek immediate care for: new chest pain, increasing dyspnea, rapid weight gain (>2 lbs/day), new edema, syncope, palpitations, ICD discharge (if implanted).',
