low speed — like walking through your living room. No incline. Just 5 minutes. You can stop at any time. You are in control. If you feel okay after 5 minutes, we can keep going. If not, we stop and try again next time. There is no failure here — just showing up is a victory.\"\n\n**6. Address the friend\'s story**:\n\"I am sorry about your friend. That must have been frightening and I can see why it is on your mind. What I can tell you is that the risk of a cardiac event during supervised cardiac rehabilitation is extremely low — approximately 1 event per 117,000 patient-hours of exercise. You are far safer exercising here under monitoring than you are being sedentary at home.\"\n\n**7. Build confidence over time**:\n\"Each session, we will gradually increase the intensity. By the end of this program, most patients tell me they cannot believe they were ever afraid. You are going to get stronger. And I will be right here with you every step of the way.\"\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Acute anxiety management, validation, concrete safety data, patient education, patient autonomy/control, graduated exposure (behavioral technique), building self-efficacy, therapeutic alliance.',
